// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SIGN ON THE DOOR (2026-09-30)
//
// This generator had NO $meta at all until now, which meant three things worth
// naming: the listing page and every share card showed an AUTOMATIC SCREENSHOT of
// whatever the terminal happened to be displaying, the browser tab took its title
// from the file, and nothing told a visitor what kind of place this is before they
// opened it. All three are wrong for this one. Patrikios, 2026-09-30: "This site
// is 18+ NSFW by default."
//
// So the title carries the 18+, the description says it in the first four words,
// the tags say it to the listing's own filter, and the IMAGE is a picture of the
// door rather than a live screenshot - an auto-screenshot of an adult terminal's
// log is a public image that nobody chose. The image is a generated CRT screen
// (uploads.dev; regenerate from the prompt in the chronicle entry if it is ever
// lost).
//
// $meta is the one thing in this file that is not a perchance list: the engine
// reads it for the listing card, the tab title and the share card. Keep the
// values on one line each - everything after the `=` is the value, verbatim.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
$meta
  title = SIA TERMINAL (18+)
  description = 18+ NSFW BY DEFAULT. An adults-only terminal you talk to: LIA, her roles, her moods and her memory, in black and green. Everything made here is creative, allegorical, symbolic and fictional - grown adults writing, playing and imagining together - and there is no content of any kind involving anyone under 18. A simple 18+ confirmation stands at the door.
  image = https://user.uploads.dev/file/9541fdc98e4a2668d937b11be65e7647.jpg
  tags = ai chat, roleplay, creative writing, terminal, adults only, 18+, nsfw

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SHOT LISTS (the idea dimensions)
//
// Everything below this line is a LIST, and every list is one dimension of a
// picture: who is in it, where they are, and - the half a filmmaker cares about
// - the film language that describes the shot. `src/js/idea.js` draws one item
// from a dimension at a time and glues the draws into a prompt, which is what
// makes a whole prompt rollable in passes instead of typed: roll the scene, look
// at it, roll the camera and the light around it, then re-roll the one dimension
// that is wrong and leave the rest exactly where they are.
//
// The lists are perchance lists rather than JSON on purpose: perchance's own
// engine is the randomizer. An item may branch (`{blonde|brunette}`), take odds
// (`^8`), or be computed (`[2+2] legs`), a whole item can carry its own `^`
// weight, and a multi-line item is glued back into a ", "-separated chain by
// `joinFragments`. That is the `^`/`{`/`[]` machinery the terminal would have to
// reinvent in JS, so the lists live here and `src/js/idea.js` reads them off
// `root`. Add a dimension by adding a list here plus a part in `idea.js`; add a
// die to the `dice` list below and a button appears for it.
//
// The lists, the curation rule (the Minoan Aegean, 1500 BCE, in-period material
// only) and the dice are ported byte-for-byte from Patrikios' standalone image
// generator ("Kali Malindra and the Cult of Fire", perchance.org/i8ixqmhq2p),
// with its comment block kept intact - the deprecated-terms rule and the
// "every adult, in every combination" rule are the spec, and they are easier to
// keep if they travel with the lists. That generator is left untouched; the
// lists are now this terminal's too.
//
// The imports stay at the very top, above the banner, because a generator's
// top-level names are what the engine puts on `root` - keep them apart from the
// dimensions so a list named `title` or `style` can never be mistaken for one.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

kv = {import:kv-plugin}
generateText = {import:ai-text-plugin}
generateImage = {import:text-to-image-plugin}
superFetch = {import:super-fetch-plugin}
background = {import:background-image-plugin}
// THE PAIR ROAD'S SERVER (src/js/pair.js is its client, and the server itself is the
// text/x-server-plugin element in index.html). Imported 2026-09-30 for DEVICE SYNC - two of Patrikios'
// own devices finding each other by a shared code, with no login, no Drive and nothing durable on the
// server. See THE PAIR in src/README.md and TODO.md 1dp. Nothing else in the terminal imports it, and
// nothing opens a socket until he asks for one.
createServerSocket = {import:server-plugin}
// THE GATE QR ROAD'S RELAY (src/js/gatekeeper.js rendezvous). Short-lived editable text files stand
// in as the scan-to-approve channel: unguessable name, two-minute life, burned after one use.
uploadPlugin = {import:upload-plugin}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE WALL (the background image)
//
// Patrikios, 2026-09-30, handing over the lists: the background-image-plugin
// plus a `bgData` list whose size, repeat and filter ROLL, so the wall is a
// different roll of the same picture on every load. The plugin is one line:
// it returns a single `<div style="position:fixed; inset:0; z-index:-1000;
// background-image:url(...)">`, which is exactly why it works here without
// fighting anything - everything in this terminal (the stage, both decks) is
// transparent over `body { background:#000 }`, so a wall at z -1000 shows
// through the whole page and the 18+ door (opaque, z 2000) still hides it.
//
// TWO THINGS ABOUT HIS CODE ARE DELIBERATE, AND ONE IS A PLUGIN BUG:
//
//  1. THE URL IS NOT THE ONE HE WROTE. He wrote `http://i.imgur.com/...`, and
//     the plugin's own docs say Imgur is deleting old images now. Worse, an
//     http:// image on this https page is blocked as MIXED CONTENT - the wall
//     would have been silently blank, which reads as "the plugin is broken".
//     So that exact picture was fetched once, byte-for-byte, and re-hosted on
//     perchance's own upload service; the URL below is now the source of
//     record. To change the picture: upload the new one, swap the url.
//  2. `opacity = 0.8` IS STATED IN THE LIST AND PASSED AT THE CALL SITE.
//     The plugin reads `bgData.opacity` only as a truthiness gate and then
//     emits `opacity:${opacity||1}` - the FUNCTION ARGUMENT, not the list
//     field. So `[background(bgData)]` alone renders the wall at opacity 1
//     and 0.8 does nothing. The list keeps his 0.8 as the statement of what
//     the wall should be; index.html passes it as the second argument so it
//     actually takes effect. (Verified in the live DOM, not read and assumed.)
//
// AND THE MEASUREMENT WORTH KNOWING BEFORE BLAMING ANYTHING: this picture is a
// bright high-key one - mean luminance 0.71, with 80% of its pixels above 0.5 -
// so the invert(0%) roll is a LIGHT wall behind green #00ff00 text (weak
// contrast), while the invert(100%) roll is dark (mean 0.29, half the frame
// below 0.2) and reads the way a terminal wants. Both rolls are his; the
// numbers are here so the bright one is a known trade, not a surprise.
//
// THIS PICTURE IS A PLACEHOLDER, AND THE WALL SHIPS OFF. Patrikios, 2026-09-30:
// "That image is just a placeholder image for the moment. We can have a list
// pointing to a perchance.org/upload file later. Toggle/Disable for now. But it
// is an option later is needed." So the call in index.html stays wired and the
// switch is elsewhere: the `wallOff` class on <body> in index.html plus one CSS
// rule, driven by src/js/wall.js (`/wall on|off|toggle|forget`, the choice kept
// in this browser). Off is the shipped state, so the measurement above is what
// to expect WHEN HE TURNS IT ON, not what is on screen now.
//
// WHAT "LATER" LOOKS LIKE, so it is not re-derived: a LIST of URLs, each one a
// perchance.org/upload file, and `url = imageUrl` - the plugin's own branch for
// a list (it checks getPropertyNames and selects one item) covers it, so the
// roll becomes per-load across several pictures with no other change. AND THE
// TAKEAWAY HE NAMED: perchance.org/upload is the free, permanent home for an
// asset inside this ecosystem - it is where this picture already lives (the
// Imgur URL he pasted was only ever a placeholder source), it is why the $meta
// image is on uploads.dev, and it is the road to prefer for anything the
// terminal should still have in a year. /wall help says the short version.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

bgData
  url = https://user.uploads.dev/file/db8547df143abbb7649cc1e2de30932b.jpeg
  size = {cover|contain^0.5|auto|{50-500}px^2}
  repeat = {no-repeat|repeat}
  position = center
  opacity = 0.8
  filter = invert({0|100}%) blur({0^3|10}px) hue-rotate({0-100}deg)


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The idea lists: the predetermined source of every prompt this generator
// offers. Each list is one dimension of an image, and the 🎲 buttons draw one
// item from each, gluing them into a finished prompt (see `PARTS` at the top of
// src/js/idea.js for the order and the glue, and `dice` below for which button
// rolls which dimensions).
//
//   subject     who              -> the positive prompt
//   character   a named person   -> the positive prompt (replaces subject/age/physique)
//   age         how old          -> the positive prompt
//   physique    body/build       -> the positive prompt
//   expression  the face         -> the positive prompt
//   setting     where (location) -> the positive prompt
//   space       interior/out     -> the positive prompt
//   props       the rig          -> the positive prompt
//   act         pose or action   -> the positive prompt (one branch of the two lists)
//   pose        where the bodies -> the positive prompt (via `act`, or on its own)
//   timeOfDay   when             -> the positive prompt
//   weather     the air          -> the positive prompt
//   cropping    the frame        -> the positive prompt
//   shot        framing          -> the positive prompt
//   angle       camera angle     -> the positive prompt
//   camera      shot on what     -> the positive prompt
//   capture     the file/format  -> the positive prompt
//   lens        lens/focal length-> the positive prompt
//   depthOfField focus           -> the positive prompt
//   lighting    light            -> the positive prompt
//   colorGrade  graded how       -> the positive prompt
//   lut         imitation look   -> the positive prompt
//   style       rendered how     -> the positive prompt
//   negativePrompt  what to steer away from -> the *negative* field
//
// Because the dimensions are independent, one chip can be re-rolled on its own
// while the others stay put — that is how a subject, a setting or a camera is
// kept consistent across a run of images.
//
// The lists are the whole idea space: add, delete or reorder items freely, and
// add a dimension by adding a list here plus an entry in `idea.parts`.
//
// CURATION RULE: everything in `subject`, `character`, `setting`, `pose`,
// `action` and `props` stays inside the Minoan Aegean. **1500 BCE is the
// standard date** — the `setting`
// chains hard-code "Late Bronze Age / 1500 BCE" and that is what ships, even
// though the Minoan period as a whole spans roughly 3500-1100 BCE. Nothing else
// creeps in: not 3500 BCE, not the classical Greek world. No modern places,
// clothing, props or roles either: "pharmacy", "back room", "laboratory",
// "headset", "name tag", "polo shirt", "e-stim" and their relatives are
// deprecated and must not come back.
//
// WHO IS ALLOWED: every adult is, in every combination. `subject`, `pose` and
// `action` are as explicit as the user wants them to be, and the whole range is
// permitted — lesbian and gay pairings, bisexual and mixed-gender groupings, and
// group scenes that mix adults of different ages. "Mixed age" means a group of
// adults (18+), never a minor: `age` stays 18-and-up and the negative list still
// excludes children. Nothing here is fenced off as a special case, so write a new
// item wherever the combination belongs rather than giving it a list of its own.
//
// SEX TOYS: a phallus, a dildo, a plug or a harness is in-period *when it is made
// of in-period material* — turned olive wood, polished horn, carved stone,
// knotted leather, bone. Same rule as `props`: no plastic, no silicone, no steel
// or machined metal, no modern look. Write the object a Minoan workshop would
// actually have made ("a carved olive-wood phallus in a knotted leather
// harness"), never a modern sex-shop product.
//
// The CINEMATIC dimensions — `space`, `timeOfDay`, `weather`, `cropping`,
// `shot`, `angle`, `camera`, `capture`, `lens`, `depthOfField`, `lighting`,
// `colorGrade`, `lut` and `style` —
// are the other half of the tool and ARE deliberately modern: this is film
// language, not a description of what exists in the scene. Every still is a
// plate that a motion picture tool animates next, so it is described the way a
// shot is described on a call sheet: framing, angle, lens, body, focus, light,
// time, weather and crop. Keep additions to those lists to shot vocabulary (an
// in-period scene tag belongs in `setting`), and keep modern *scenes* (backrooms,
// cyberpunk, neon anime) out of all of them.
//
// `age`, `physique` and `expression` are the ACTOR dimensions: generic casting
// and direction vocabulary, independent of the `subject` presets, so age, build
// or expression can be held or re-rolled while a subject stays recognisable.
// They describe a performer, not a scene, so they may say modern things only in
// the sense that a call sheet would ("athletic frame", "eyes closed in bliss");
// in-period *costume* still belongs in `subject` or `setting`. `age` is
// adult-only (18 and up) — the negative list already excludes children.
//
// `props` is the one exception on the scene side: it is restraint furniture and
// equipment (timber frame slings, rope harnesses, bronze rings, sheepskin-lined
// cuffs), so it must stay in-period like `setting` — no modern hardware.
//
// `style` is the canonical look for every roll, the way `negativePrompt` is the
// canonical anti-look: it is the long "hyperreal cinematic master" tail that
// closes every prompt. It is deliberately a one-item list, so re-rolling it is a
// no-op until someone adds a second alternative.
//
// The dice that roll these dimensions in groups — `d6` for the scene, `d20` for
// everything else, and as many more as you care to add — are the `dice` list
// below.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One fragment per line
//
// Perchance reads `[`, `{` and `^` inside a list item as syntax, so a single
// long, comma-separated item can use none of it. Split that chain onto its own
// lines and every fragment becomes its own item, free to branch
// (`{blonde|brunette}`), take odds (`{blonde|brunette^9}`) or be computed
// (`[2+2] legs`). `$output = [joinFragments(this)]` glues the lines back into
// the same ", "-separated chain the prompt expects, and a whole alternative can
// still carry its own `^` weight. Sibling lines are always included, so a `^`
// on a fragment line by itself does nothing.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

joinFragments(node) =>
  let parts = [node.getName, node.joinItems(", ")].filter(Boolean);
  return parts.join(", ");

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The dice
//
// The 🎲 buttons. A die rolls a *group* of the dimensions below, so a scene can
// be built in passes instead of in one go: roll the small die for the bones of
// the shot, look at it, then roll the big die for the film language around it —
// and press either one again to re-roll just that half. A die only touches its
// own dimensions; whatever the other dice put there stays exactly as it is, so
// `d6` then `d20` reads as one prompt built in two steps.
//
// The die's name is its button, and its children are the dimensions it rolls:
//
//   d6    the scene — who is in it and where they are, in six dimensions
//   d20   everything the other dice did not claim
//
// A die with **no children** is a catch-all: it takes whatever no other die has
// claimed, and a die that names its dimensions always wins over it, whatever the
// order they are written in. That is what makes a third die free — uncomment
// `d4` below, or add a `d100`, and `d20` shrinks to fit without a line being
// edited anywhere else. The number is the die's name, not a promise: hover a die
// button to see the count and the dimensions it actually rolls.
//
// `🎲 idea` rolls every dimension at once, `🎬 scene` does the same while keeping
// the character, and a chip's ↻ re-rolls the one dimension it names.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

dice
  d6
    subject
    character
    setting
    space
    props
    act
  d20

// More dice are just more lines. This one makes the light its own button, and
// `d20` gives up those four dimensions automatically, because a catch-all only
// ever takes what is left:
//
//   d4
//     lighting
//     colorGrade
//     lut
//     style

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The naming glossary: English -> myth register
//
// The MYTH line (the name read out of a finished picture — src/myth.js) is asked
// to speak this language. Each line is one English term and the words this cycle
// reaches for instead, so the tool holds a *translation* rather than a bag of
// archaic nouns:
//
//   sex: the rites, the sacred union
//
// which is what lets a still be named "Two Fire-Walkers Drink Deep at The Font of
// Nectar" instead of "two women having sex". Add lines freely — the list is the
// whole glossary, and a term with no line yet is simply an English word the
// namer has to invent around. A line may also carry words with no English term
// in front of it (`the labyrinth`), which is just a word to reach for.
//
// Keep the left-hand terms plain and unglamorous: they are the *lookup* keys.

mythGlossary
  fire: the sacred flame, ember, ash, the hearth, the bull-fire
  hearth: the mother of the house, the feeding stone
  light: the god's glance, the gold hour
  water: the spring, the sea, the salt, the god's tears
  garden: the god's plot, the tended green, the orchard of the sanctuary
  garden work: watering, tending, the long green labour
  bull: the horned god, the bull, the labyrinth's keeper
  labyrinth: the winding house, the coiled path
  priest-king: the masked one, the bull-king, the horned crown
  priestess: the fire-tender, the veiled one, the god's mouth
  sex: the rites, the sacred union, the coupling, the god's work
  oral sex: drinking, tasting, taking the god into the mouth, drinking deep
  penetration: the horn entering the vessel, the god filling his vessel
  ejaculation: the outpouring, the god's gift released, the flood
  semen: seed, nectar, honey, the god's gift
  breasts: the amphora, the honey jars, the twin vessels
  nipples: the stops of the honey jars, the buds
  vulva: the fig, the shell, the sacred gate, the font
  buttocks: the twin hills, the harvest mounds, the full moon
  hips: the wide valley, the threshing floor
  thighs: the gateposts, the pillars at the threshold
  mouth: the font of nectar, the speaking gate
  tongue: the flame that tastes
  fingers: the fire-tenders' fingers, the five waxing lights
  hands: the hands that tend the flame
  hair: the dark harvest, the rope of night
  skin: the pelt, the oiled hide, the sun's work
  freckles: the god's scattered seed, the speckled gift
  tan: the sun's mark, the harvest colour
  sweat: the labour's dew, the salt of the rite
  oil: the anointing, the gleaming gift of the olive
  nudity: bare as the harvest, stripped for the rite
  restrained: bound for the god, roped to the pillar
  rope: the god's cord, the binding
  wood: the timber, the frame, the press
  blood: the red offering, the life-gift
  wine: the dark blood of the vine, the pressed grape
  honey: the god's sweetness, the golden gift
  moon: the night's eye, the silver witness
  dawn: the first light, the god's waking
  night: the dark house, the unlit hour
  orgasm: the breaking wave, the fire taken up
  the sacred flame
  the double axe
  the vine, the press

